Recommendation draft:

1. **3.1 readiness** is where it runs — once per run, before any piece starts, beside readiness.py, harness_health.py and doctor. It does not create a new step: 3.1 already requires an information check of "a memory folder" and its structure; memory-check is the script that proves that line for a git project.
2. On a **pass**, one readiness line, carry on. On **not applicable**, same, and do not create `project-memory/` to make the tool useful. On **needs a reviewer**, list it; only a piece that relies on that memory waits for EJ's review. On **could not run**, record unknown; no stop; the old "unverified" path (a reading piece or the entry itself) covers it. On **fail**, an unclear spot of kind decision in the 3.3 batch, provisional "carry on, name it in the state and the report; the dependent piece waits or a preparation piece fixes it" — not a gate on the whole run; a missing AGENTS.md is the one fail that holds back agent-dispatched pieces.
3. **Not per node**, **not at end of run**, **not session-start only**. Per node repeats a project-level check, and C5's staged-state snapshot means it sees no new entry until staged; end-of-run re-run is only worth it if the run staged/committed its memory, else it passes on the old snapshot. Session-start `--warn` is the same result delivered to every session; it is a harness hook, not a method step, and waits for EJ's yes.
4. **Redundant / must not do**: nothing in the method is replaced. The 3.1 information check is not just guideline conformance; §4's per-node application and reviewer lists remain. The tool must not judge reviewer items, must not replace memory_index.py, must not require project-memory or git, must not turn its exit code into the run's stop (the method must work in a plain directory and a repo with no project-memory), and must not be recorded as "memory checked" when items need a reviewer or could not run.

Cost/risk:
- A false confidence: 72 n/a on this repo; 3 reviewer items; exit 0 can look "clean" (smoke-report; R3 counts MUST only, CACHES has no level). Risk: the step is recorded as a pass when most of the memory was never examined. Name it.
- A new stop by the back door: if a failing MUST stops the run, every project with a small memory break pays a repair before any work; that's a step the owner didn't ask for (#5, #8).
- Snapshot mismatch: staged-state check (C5) vs working tree. A check at the wrong moment passes on memory that isn't the memory the node will read. This is a technical risk of end-of-run/per-node.
- Session hook: one line at every session in every git project without AGENTS.md; noise if installed before memory exists; and output becomes session context. Still owner's yes.
- A reviewer gap: 3 reviewer items go to EJ; if the method just notes them, the run can finish with "memory checked" but 3 unjudged.
- Cost: running `--warn` runs every check (R2), so session start pays the full check each time; the tool is read-only, no writes.

How we would know I was right:
- A test/measure: take a project with project-memory, plant one break per checkable requirement (U2's minimum acceptance), run a designed run end-to-end: the break appears in the readiness row at 3.1 and the run either carries a named note or a preparation piece fixes it; no run stops on not applicable/could not run; no node runs memory-check again. Count: steps added = 0 (it is inside 3.1), checks added = 1 readiness line. If within 3 runs it catches no break the method would have missed and adds a turn, the placement is wrong → move it to session-start hook only. Also: if a failing MUST at 3.1 delays a run whose pieces never touch that memory, the handling rule is wrong. That's a concrete observable.

