# 孫子 (Sun Tzu) — where memory-check belongs in the workflow-design method

## Answer

Run it **once, in 3.1 Readiness** (the skill's step 1), before any piece starts — not per node, not as a separate end-of-run pass, and not at session start only. It fills the one line 3.1 already asks about memory; a failing or unjudged result tells the design what memory it may stand on, and holds only the pieces that rely on that memory, not the run.

Two sentences. Good.

## How I decided

1. **The moment is the reckoning before the march (my #1, quoted).** 「多算勝，少算不勝，而況於無算乎？」 The method already puts its read-only proofs at 3.1 — `readiness.py`, `harness_health.py`, `doctor` (`docs/WORKFLOW_DESIGN_METHOD.md` 102–110). Starting a run on memory nothing has counted is the "no count" case. So the check belongs at that gate, before 3.2, not after the work.

2. **Fix the half you own (my #2, quoted).** 「先為不可勝，以待敵之可勝。」 A run cannot make a project's memory correct by starting; it can know before it starts which part of that memory it may stand on. That is what the tool gives, and why the result belongs in readiness rather than in a worker's good character later.

3. **Name the empty half of 3.1 (my #4, quoted; my reading of the method).** 「知彼知己，百戰不殆」 — but 3.1 knows the tools, the harness and the hooks, and asks about "a memory folder" in prose with no script (lines 113–129). memory-check is that script for the part it covers: locations, the guidelines' checkable requirements, AGENTS.md. It does not answer whether the knowledge *this task* needs is there; that remains 3.1's judgement. So it is one line made checkable, not a new step.

4. **Make the addition pay for itself (my #5, quoted).** 「合於利而動，不合於利而止。」 It pays only if it adds no step and does not gate every run. So: one run, one readiness line; the report, not the exit code, is the result. On this repo that line is "0 fail, 3 pass (the tool's own F1–F3), 3 need a reviewer, 72 not applicable, 0 could not run" (`runs/.../nodes/smoke-report.md` 128) — most of the memory was never examined, and a cache break would not even change the exit code (CACHES has no level; smoke-report 129). A green line here is a count, not a pass.

5. **Map each state to a decision, not to a stop.** (table)

| state | what 3.1 does | who acts |
|---|---|---|
| pass | record the counts; carry on | readiness |
| fail (a MUST) | a decision spot in the 3.3 batch; provisional: carry on, name it in the state and the report; only pieces that read or write that memory wait (or a preparation piece repairs it) | EJ; in a time-boxed run, the experts (3.8 item 14) |
| needs a reviewer | never counted as a pass; listed in the readiness row and state; only the piece that relies on that entry waits for the reviewer | owner or the person they name (SoT) |
| not applicable | carry on; no `project-memory/` is not a failure (C4) and is not a reason to create it | none |
| could not run | record "unknown" with the reason (shallow clone; or not a git root, where the tool exits 2 before checking); no stop; a piece that needs that memory reads the entry or gets a small check | readiness + the piece |

6. **Do not repeat a project-level fact per node (my #8, quoted).** 「兵貴勝，不貴久。」 The snapshot is the staged state (C5), so between nodes the memory the tool sees does not change; per-node runs buy duration, not a new fact. The end-of-run pass is the same snapshot problem: it can pass on the memory as it was, not as a node wrote it. The one exception is a node that commits memory: it runs the tool on its own commit as its check (3.6/§4), not as a sixth step.

7. **Use 勢, not a reminder (my #12, quoted).** 「善戰者，求之於勢，不責於人。」 A sentence in each brief asking the node to check memory is 責於人; the `--warn` hook is 勢 — the same result in front of every session. But the hook is a harness setting and waits for EJ's yes (`settings-diff.md`: "Yes recorded: (not yet)"). The method's placement stays 3.1; the hook is how sessions outside a designed run get the same warning.

8. **Not every road is the run's road (my #13, quoted).** 「途有所不由」 — a break in memory the run never reads is not a reason to stop the run. Only the dependent piece waits; the report carries the rest. This is also what keeps the placement from adding the repair step the owner did not ask for.

9. **Name the general's fault the check can hide (my #15, quoted).** 「凡此五者，將之過也」 — the tool's own five dangers are a green exit 0 with 72 n/a and 3 reviewer items, and a readiness line that says "memory OK". The placement must make that impossible: the counts and the reviewer list go in the line; "memory OK" is never written.

Wait, "the tool's own five dangers" is a stretch — #15 is about the general's five dangers. I can say "the fault to guard is the general's kind: a clean-looking result read as victory". Better: "A clean result read as a pass is the general's fault #15 names — the fault is ours, not the ground's." That's my reading. Fine: label "(my #15, quoted; my reading of its application)".

10. **A checked thing must be able to fail (my #17, quoted).** 「策之而知得失之計，作之而知動靜之理」 — probe it by planting a break. The test below is that probe; the placement is right only if the break shows at 3.1 and the run changes only where it must.

That's 10 steps — maybe too many. The brief wants numbered steps; 8-9 is fine. Could merge 8/9. Let me keep 8: 1,2,3,4,5 (table),6,7,8,9? That's 9. Fine.

Actually step 3 is long; okay.

The result table should tie to the five states. Good.

One issue: the "fail" row says "a decision spot in the 3.3 batch" — 3.1 comes before 3.3; the spot is recorded and goes into 3.3's list. That's consistent: 3.3 lists all unclear spots before resolving any; but 3.1's readiness happens before 3.2/3.3. The method's order is 3.0 → 3.1 → 3.2 → 3.3. A failing memory found at 3.1 becomes an unclear spot carried into 3.3. Fine.

For "needs a reviewer": the method's 3.1 blueprint statuses don't cover it; but the tool's own result state says so. A reviewer item is not a "fail"; it's a known unknown. The piece depending on it is exactly 3.6's "a result is verified before anything that depends on it starts". Good.

## What I need to know

1. **Does a failing MUST stop a run, or only hold the pieces that need that memory?** My answer assumes the second; if EJ wants a gate, the placement is still 3.1 but its result handling changes from a decision spot to a stop.
2. **Will the session-start hook be installed?** If yes, the warning reaches every session and the 3.1 run is for the run's own record; if no, designed runs are the only place it runs. (The method step is the same either way.)
3. **Which projects will use the method — git repositories with `project-memory/`, or plain directories?** This repo has none: the tool reports 72 n/a. On a plain directory it exits 2 (F2). If most projects are like this, 3.1 records "not applicable" and the hook is the only real value; if most have memory, 3.1 is the real check.
4. **Who reviews the "needs a reviewer" items, and by when?** If no one is named, those items are noise and the readiness line should list ids only; if EJ names a reviewer, the dependent pieces wait for them.
5. **Will a run commit the `project-memory/` entries it writes?** If yes, the writing node's check on its own commit is cheap and catches a stale index; if no, an end check reads the old staged snapshot and should not run.

Maybe 4 is enough; 5 is okay. Each changes the answer. The brief says "questions whose answers would change the answer; 'none' if none". I'll list 4-5.

## Costs and risks

Intro: my #6 (quoted 「智者之慮，必雜於利害」) — the harm side, before the benefit.

- **A green that is not coverage (my #4, half-known; smoke-report 128–129).** On this repo: 0 fail, 72 n/a, 3 need a reviewer, 0 could not run. A readiness line that says "pass" claims more than the tool knows. *Price:* the method records a check it did not get; the fix is to print counts and reviewer ids, never "memory OK".
- **A gate by the back door (my #5, quoted).** If a fail stops the run, every project with a memory break pays a repair before any work — a step the owner did not ask for, and the largest cost of the placement. *Price:* the method gets slower exactly where memory is imperfect.
- **A check at the wrong snapshot (my reading; C5).** The tool reads the staged state, not the working tree. Per-node and end-of-run runs can pass on the memory as it was, not as the node wrote it. *Price:* false assurance, and the reason 3.1 (before work) is the safe moment.
- **A tool that cannot run where the method must (F2; method 3.1 line 94).** The method must work in a plain directory; the tool exits 2 outside a git root. If that is read as a fail, readiness gains a requirement the method rejects. *Price:* a usage error mistaken for a memory break.
- **Warning noise at session start (settings-diff; my #8).** The proposed hook prints one line in every git project without `AGENTS.md`, at each session, and its output becomes context. *Price:* if installed before a project has memory, it warns about a thing the project never had; it is also the owner's unrecorded yes.
- **The reviewer's three (my #15; VISION, C1/C6).** Three items need a person; the tool cannot judge them (by design). *Price:* if the run notes them and no one reviews, the memory the nodes relied on was never judged; the check catches the need, not the judgement.

Maybe 5-6 bullets.

## How we would know I was right

A planted-break test (my #17):

- Fixture project with `project-memory/` and one planted break per checkable requirement (U2's minimum acceptance), plus a second run that never reads the broken entry. At 3.1 the break appears in the readiness line; the run's report names it; the pieces that do not read it finish; no new node is added; the writing node's check, if the run commits memory, catches a stale INDEX.
- On this repo (no project-memory), the 3.1 line is "not applicable" with the counts; the run is not stopped; the hook prints nothing (I ran it: exit 0, no output).
- On a plain directory (no git), the tool is skipped with a note, not counted as a fail; the run proceeds.
- Measure over the first three designs: steps added by the placement = 0; checks added = 1 readiness line; pieces held = only those that read the broken memory; false passes = 0 (no "memory OK" when reviewer/n-a items exist); time added at 3.1 ≤ the hook's 20 s timeout.
- If after three designs it caught no break the method would have missed and added a COO turn, the placement is wrong: fall back to the session-start hook only. If it caught a break in memory a node read and the node proceeded anyway, the "hold the dependent piece" rule failed.

That's a concrete test and measure.

